Documentation section docs/searching.md#Searching > Matching:

`matches(book, query)` answers whether one book satisfies a parsed query. It
ignores case, so `dune` finds *Dune*; pass `case_sensitive=True` when that
matters.

The chunk bookshelf/search.py::matches before the change:

def matches(book, query, case_sensitive=False):
    haystack = f'{book.title} {book.author}'
    if not case_sensitive:
        haystack = haystack.lower()

    def present(term):
        return (term if case_sensitive else term.lower()) in haystack
    if not all((present(term) for term in query.required)):
        return False
    return not query.alternatives or any((present(term) for term in query.alternatives))

And after it:

def matches(book, query, case_sensitive=True):
    haystack = f'{book.title} {book.author}'
    if not case_sensitive:
        haystack = haystack.lower()

    def present(term):
        return (term if case_sensitive else term.lower()) in haystack
    if not all((present(term) for term in query.required)):
        return False
    return not query.alternatives or any((present(term) for term in query.alternatives))

What the change made wrong: `matches()` no longer ignores case by default; `case_sensitive` now defaults to `True`, so the documentation’s description of the default behavior is wrong.

The section as the repair would leave it:

`matches(book, query)` answers whether one book satisfies a parsed query. By default it is case sensitive, so `dune` will not find *Dune*; pass `case_sensitive=False` when you want it to ignore case.
